module.exports = {
  title: 'Performance & Advanced Patterns',
  slug: 'performance-advanced',
  order: 8,
  topics: [
    {
      title: 'Performance Optimisation',
      slug: 'performance-optimization',
      order: 0,
      content: [
        `<h2>Why it matters</h2>`,
        `<p>"How would you optimise a slow Vue app?" is a staple senior question, and the answer that impresses is not a list of tricks — it is a <em>method</em>. Measure first, identify which of the three costs you are paying (bundle, render, update), fix that one, measure again. Candidates who lead with "I'd add <code>v-once</code> everywhere" are optimising blind.</p>`,
        `<p>It matters practically because the biggest wins are almost never micro-optimisations. Shipping 800 kB of JavaScript to a phone on 4G costs seconds; a slightly inefficient computed costs microseconds. Knowing the order of magnitude of each fix is the skill.</p>`,
        `<h2>The concept, simply</h2>`,
        `<p>Vue performance has three distinct costs, and they need different fixes:</p>`,
        `<table>`,
        `<thead><tr><th>Cost</th><th>Symptom</th><th>Main levers</th></tr></thead>`,
        `<tbody>`,
        `<tr><td><strong>Load</strong></td><td>Slow first paint, blank screen</td><td>Code splitting, tree-shaking, dependency audit, compression</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td><strong>Render</strong></td><td>Slow initial display of a page or list</td><td>Virtual scrolling, pagination, fewer components, lighter markup</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td><strong>Update</strong></td><td>Laggy typing, janky interaction</td><td>Narrower reactivity, <code>shallowRef</code>, <code>v-memo</code>, debouncing</td></tr>`,
        `</tbody>`,
        `</table>`,
        `{{fig:ch9-performance-costs.svg|Three-part diagram of Vue performance costs — load cost from bundle size, render cost from component count, and update cost from reactivity work — each with its own set of remedies|Diagnose which cost you're paying before choosing a fix; the remedies don't transfer between them.}}`,
        `<p>The order matters. Load cost dominates on real networks and is usually the cheapest to fix. Update cost is where micro-optimisations live and is usually the least valuable place to start.</p>`,
        `<h2>How it works</h2>`,
        `<p><strong>Measure first.</strong> Four tools, each answering a different question: Lighthouse for real-world load metrics; <code>rollup-plugin-visualizer</code> for what is actually in the bundle; Vue DevTools' Performance timeline for which components render and how often; and the Chrome Performance panel for where frame time goes. Guessing wastes effort — the classic outcome is a day spent memoising a component that renders twice.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Load cost.</strong> Route-level code splitting is the highest-value change in most applications (Chapter 7.4). After that, audit dependencies: a date library, a lodash default import, or a charting library pulled in eagerly often outweighs all your own code. Prefer per-function imports, check whether a dependency has a lighter alternative, and make sure the server sends Brotli or gzip.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Render cost</strong> is dominated by <em>how many components and DOM nodes</em> you create. A ten-thousand-row table is slow because it is ten thousand rows, not because the diff is inefficient. Virtual scrolling — rendering only the visible window — turns that into thirty rows and is the single most effective fix for long lists. Pagination is the simpler alternative when the UX allows.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Update cost</strong> comes from reactivity doing more work than necessary:</p>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Deep reactivity on large structures.</strong> A <code>ref</code> holding 50,000 rows proxies every one on first read. <code>shallowRef</code> skips that when you always replace the array wholesale.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Methods where computeds belong.</strong> A method in a template re-runs on every re-render; a computed re-runs only when its dependencies change.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Deep watchers.</strong> <code>{ deep: true }</code> traverses the whole structure on every check. Watch a specific path instead.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Component granularity.</strong> The component is the update boundary, so a single large component re-renders entirely when any of its state changes. Splitting it narrows the blast radius.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>High-frequency events.</strong> <code>scroll</code>, <code>resize</code>, <code>mousemove</code> and keystroke-driven search need debouncing or throttling.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<p><strong><code>v-once</code> and <code>v-memo</code></strong> are the explicit escape hatches. <code>v-once</code> renders a subtree once and never updates it. <code>v-memo="[a, b]"</code> skips updating a subtree unless one of the listed values changed — genuinely useful on large lists, and close to useless elsewhere. Both are last resorts, because a stale-looking UI is a worse bug than a slow one.</p>`,
        `<h2>Code examples</h2>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// ── Load: split, and audit what you import ──\n// Route level\n{ path: '/reports', component: () =&gt; import('@/views/Reports.vue') }\n\n// Component level\nconst Chart = defineAsyncComponent(() =&gt; import('@/components/Chart.vue'))\n\n// ❌ Pulls in the whole library\nimport _ from 'lodash'\nimport moment from 'moment'          // ~70 kB, all locales\n\n// ✅ Import only what you use, or pick a lighter dependency\nimport debounce from 'lodash-es/debounce'\nimport { format } from 'date-fns'    // tree-shakeable\n// or Intl.DateTimeFormat — zero bytes, built into the browser</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;!-- ── Render: virtualise long lists ── --&gt;\n&lt;script setup&gt;\nimport { useVirtualList } from '@vueuse/core'\n\nconst { list, containerProps, wrapperProps } = useVirtualList(allRows, {\n  itemHeight: 48,\n  overscan: 10,\n})\n&lt;/script&gt;\n\n&lt;template&gt;\n  &lt;!-- 10,000 rows in state, ~30 in the DOM --&gt;\n  &lt;div v-bind="containerProps" class="h-96 overflow-auto"&gt;\n    &lt;div v-bind="wrapperProps"&gt;\n      &lt;div v-for="{ data, index } in list" :key="data.id" class="row"&gt;\n        {{ index }} — {{ data.name }}\n      &lt;/div&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// ── Update: narrow the reactivity ──\nimport { shallowRef, triggerRef, markRaw, computed } from 'vue'\n\n// ① Large dataset always replaced wholesale — skip deep proxying\nconst rows = shallowRef([])\nrows.value = await fetchRows()          // triggers\n\n// ② Third-party instances must never be proxied\nconst chart = shallowRef(markRaw(new Chart(el, config)))\n\n// ③ Computed, not a method, for derived values in templates\nconst visible = computed(() =&gt; items.value.filter(i =&gt; i.active))\n\n// ④ Watch a path, not the whole object\nwatch(() =&gt; filters.value.status, refetch)      // ✅ cheap\n// watch(filters, refetch, { deep: true })       // ❌ traverses everything\n\n// ⑤ Debounce high-frequency input\nimport { refDebounced } from '@vueuse/core'\nconst query = ref('')\nconst debouncedQuery = refDebounced(query, 300)\nwatch(debouncedQuery, search)           // one request per pause, not per keystroke</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;!-- ── The explicit escape hatches ── --&gt;\n&lt;template&gt;\n  &lt;!-- Rendered once, never updated again --&gt;\n  &lt;footer v-once&gt;\n    &lt;LegalText /&gt;   &lt;!-- expensive and genuinely static --&gt;\n  &lt;/footer&gt;\n\n  &lt;!-- Skip the update unless one of these values changed.\n       Worth it on a large list; pointless on a handful of rows. --&gt;\n  &lt;div v-for="row in rows" :key="row.id" v-memo="[row.selected, row.value]"&gt;\n    &lt;ExpensiveCell :row="row" /&gt;\n  &lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// ── Measuring, so you fix the right thing ──\n// 1. What's in the bundle?\n//    npm i -D rollup-plugin-visualizer\nimport { visualizer } from 'rollup-plugin-visualizer'\nexport default defineConfig({\n  plugins: [vue(), visualizer({ open: true, gzipSize: true })],\n})\n\n// 2. Which components re-render, and how often?\n//    Vue DevTools → Performance → record an interaction\n\n// 3. Real-world load metrics\n//    Lighthouse / PageSpeed Insights → LCP, TBT, CLS\n\n// 4. Where does frame time go?\n//    Chrome DevTools → Performance → look for long tasks</code></pre>`,
        `<h2>Common mistakes &amp; gotchas</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Optimising without measuring.</strong> The bottleneck is almost never where it feels like it is.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Reaching for <code>v-once</code>/<code>v-memo</code> first.</strong> They are last resorts with real staleness risk; splitting, virtualising and debouncing come first.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Applying React habits.</strong> Vue's per-property tracking already avoids most unnecessary re-renders; there is no <code>memo</code> to sprinkle.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Deep-watching large objects.</strong> A full traversal on every check, often to detect one field.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Calling methods in templates for derived values.</strong> Re-runs on every render including unrelated ones.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Rendering thousands of rows.</strong> No amount of diff tuning fixes the cost of creating the DOM. Virtualise or paginate.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Splitting the first route.</strong> Adds a round trip to first paint for no benefit.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Ignoring images.</strong> On many sites images dwarf the JavaScript; lazy-load, size correctly and serve modern formats before micro-optimising components.</li>`,
        `<li><strong><code>v-memo</code> with a missing dependency.</strong> The subtree silently stops updating — a stale UI with no error.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>Best practices</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Measure, fix one thing, measure again. Keep a bundle-size budget in CI.</li>`,
        `<li>Do route-level code splitting on day one — it is the highest-value change in most apps.</li>`,
        `<li>Audit dependencies regularly; one careless import can outweigh all your own code.</li>`,
        `<li>Virtualise any list that can exceed a few hundred rows.</li>`,
        `<li>Use <code>shallowRef</code> for large immutable payloads and <code>markRaw</code> for library instances.</li>`,
        `<li>Prefer computeds over methods for anything derived and rendered.</li>`,
        `<li>Watch specific paths rather than deep-watching objects.</li>`,
        `<li>Debounce or throttle high-frequency events and search inputs.</li>`,
        `<li>Split large components so state changes re-render less.</li>`,
        `<li>Treat <code>v-once</code> and <code>v-memo</code> as targeted last resorts, and document why each one is there.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>⭐ Interview lens</h2>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "An app feels slow. Walk me through diagnosing it."</strong><br>A: First I'd establish which cost we're paying. Lighthouse tells me whether it's load — a big bundle, slow LCP — and <code>rollup-plugin-visualizer</code> shows what's actually in it. If load is fine but a page is slow to appear, it's render cost, usually too many components or an unvirtualised list. If it's laggy during interaction, it's update cost, and Vue DevTools' performance timeline shows which components re-render and how often. Only then do I pick a fix, because the remedies don't transfer between the three.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "How would you render a table with 10,000 rows?"</strong><br>A: Not by rendering 10,000 rows. Virtual scrolling — render only the visible window plus a small overscan buffer, with a spacer element providing the scroll height — turns it into about thirty DOM nodes. VueUse's <code>useVirtualList</code> or vue-virtual-scroller both do this. Pagination or infinite scroll are simpler alternatives when the UX allows. The point is that the cost is creating and maintaining the nodes, so no amount of diff optimisation helps.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "When would you use <code>v-memo</code>?"</strong><br>A: Rarely, and essentially only on large lists. <code>v-memo="[a, b]"</code> skips updating that subtree unless one of the listed values changed, which pays off when each row is expensive and most rows are unchanged. It's dangerous because an omitted dependency means the subtree silently stops updating — a stale UI with no error. <code>v-once</code> is the stricter version: render once, never update. Both are last resorts after splitting, virtualising and fixing reactivity scope.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Does Vue need something like React's <code>memo</code>?" (trap)</strong><br>A: Not in the same way. React re-renders a component and its subtree whenever its state changes, so preventing that waste is the developer's job. Vue tracks dependencies per property, so a component only re-renders if it actually read something that changed — a sibling reading other fields is untouched automatically. That's why Vue codebases don't have memoisation scattered through them, and why importing that habit adds noise without benefit.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "What's the single biggest win in a typical Vue app?"</strong><br>A: Route-level code splitting, usually by a wide margin. Without it, everyone who loads the login page downloads the admin dashboard, the charts and the editor. It's one line per route and often halves the entry bundle. After that it's dependency audit — a moment.js or a full lodash import can outweigh all the application code — and then images, which on a lot of sites dwarf the JavaScript entirely.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<h2>Cheat-card</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Three costs: <strong>load</strong> (bundle) · <strong>render</strong> (node count) · <strong>update</strong> (reactivity). Different fixes.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Measure first</strong>: Lighthouse · rollup-plugin-visualizer · Vue DevTools timeline · Chrome Performance.</li>`,
        `<li>Load: route splitting → dependency audit → compression.</li>`,
        `<li>Render: <strong>virtualise</strong> long lists · paginate · fewer components.</li>`,
        `<li>Update: <code>shallowRef</code> · <code>markRaw</code> · computed not method · watch a path not <code>deep</code> · debounce · split components.</li>`,
        `<li><code>v-once</code> / <code>v-memo</code> = <strong>last resorts</strong>; a missing <code>v-memo</code> dep = silent stale UI.</li>`,
        `<li>No <code>memo</code> needed — Vue's tracking is per-property.</li>`,
        `<li>Never split the first route. Don't forget images.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
      ].join('\n'),
    },

    {
      title: 'Render Functions and JSX',
      slug: 'render-functions-jsx',
      order: 1,
      content: [
        `<h2>Why it matters</h2>`,
        `<p>Templates cover ninety-something percent of components, but the remaining few are genuinely awkward: a heading component that renders <code>h1</code> through <code>h6</code>, a component that wraps and modifies its slot children, a design-system primitive that generates markup from a schema. Render functions are the answer, and knowing when to reach for one — and when not to — is a senior-level judgement call.</p>`,
        `<p>Interviewers also use this to check that you understand templates <em>are</em> render functions. If you know that <code>&lt;div&gt;{{ x }}&lt;/div&gt;</code> compiles to <code>h('div', null, x)</code>, the whole rendering model clicks into place.</p>`,
        `<h2>The concept, simply</h2>`,
        `<p>A <strong>render function</strong> returns a vnode tree directly, using <code>h()</code> instead of a template. It is what your template becomes at build time; writing one by hand just skips the compiler.</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">import { h } from 'vue'\n\n// &lt;div class=\"card\"&gt;&lt;h1&gt;{{ title }}&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;/div&gt;\nh('div', { class: 'card' }, [\n  h('h1', null, title),\n])</code></pre>`,
        `<p><code>h(type, props, children)</code>: the type is a tag string or a component; props is an object of attributes, props and <code>onEvent</code> listeners; children is a string, an array, or an object of slot functions.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>JSX</strong> is a nicer syntax for the same thing, available in Vue via <code>@vitejs/plugin-vue-jsx</code>. It compiles to <code>h()</code> calls exactly as templates do.</p>`,
        `{{fig:ch9-render-functions.svg|Three routes to the same vnode tree: a template compiled by the SFC compiler, a hand-written h() render function, and JSX compiled by the Babel plugin|Templates, render functions and JSX all produce the same vnode tree — they differ in what the compiler can prove about them.}}`,
        `<h2>How it works</h2>`,
        `<p>In <code>&lt;script setup&gt;</code> you return a render function from <code>setup()</code> — or, more commonly, use <code>defineComponent</code> with a <code>setup</code> that returns a function. That returned function runs on every render, inside the reactive effect, so anything it reads becomes a dependency.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Props differ from templates in three ways</strong> worth memorising: event listeners are props named <code>onClick</code>, <code>onCustomEvent</code> (camelCase, <code>on</code>-prefixed); <code>class</code> and <code>style</code> accept the same object and array forms; and there is no directive syntax — <code>v-if</code> is a ternary, <code>v-for</code> is <code>.map()</code>, and <code>v-model</code> is an explicit value/handler pair.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Slots are functions.</strong> Passing children as an object — <code>{ default: () =&gt; […], footer: () =&gt; […] }</code> — is how you supply named slots, and it must be functions rather than arrays so the child controls when they are evaluated. Reading slots is <code>useSlots()</code> or the second <code>setup</code> argument.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>The trade-off</strong> is the point of the topic. Render functions give you the full power of JavaScript: loops, conditionals, recursion, dynamic tag generation, programmatic manipulation of children. What you give up is every compiler optimisation from Chapter 6.1 — static hoisting, patch flags and block trees all come from analysing a template. A hand-written render function is diffed in full. So templates are usually <em>faster</em> as well as more readable, which is why they remain the default.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>When a render function is genuinely right:</strong> dynamic element types driven by data, components that inspect or transform their slot children, recursive tree rendering, schema-driven forms, and library components that need maximum flexibility.</p>`,
        `<h2>Code examples</h2>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// The classic case: a heading whose tag is data-driven\nimport { defineComponent, h } from 'vue'\n\nexport default defineComponent({\n  name: 'AppHeading',\n  props: { level: { type: Number, default: 2 } },\n  setup(props, { slots }) {\n    // A template would need six branches; here the tag IS an expression\n    return () =&gt; h(\n      \`h\${props.level}\`,\n      { class: \`heading heading--\${props.level}\` },\n      slots.default?.(),\n    )\n  },\n})\n\n// &lt;AppHeading :level=\"3\"&gt;Section&lt;/AppHeading&gt;  →  &lt;h3 class=\"heading heading--3\"&gt;Section&lt;/h3&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// Template syntax → render function equivalents\nimport { h, Fragment } from 'vue'\n\n// v-if / v-else\nok ? h('p', 'Yes') : h('p', 'No')\n\n// v-if with nothing else — return null\nok ? h('p', 'Yes') : null\n\n// v-for\nh('ul', items.map(item =&gt; h('li', { key: item.id }, item.name)))\n\n// Multiple roots (fragment)\nh(Fragment, [h('h1', 'Title'), h('p', 'Body')])\n\n// Events are on-prefixed props\nh('button', { onClick: () =&gt; count.value++ }, 'Increment')\nh(MyComponent, { onCustomEvent: handler })\n\n// Modifiers have no syntax — do it manually\nh('form', { onSubmit: (e) =&gt; { e.preventDefault(); save() } })\n\n// class / style accept the same shapes as templates\nh('div', { class: ['card', { active: isActive }], style: { color: 'red' } })\n\n// v-model, expanded\nh('input', {\n  value: text.value,\n  onInput: (e) =&gt; { text.value = e.target.value },\n})\n\n// Named slots — an OBJECT OF FUNCTIONS, not arrays\nh(Card, null, {\n  default: () =&gt; h('p', 'Body'),\n  header: () =&gt; h('h2', 'Title'),\n})</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Recursive rendering — a case templates handle awkwardly:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// A tree renderer, arbitrarily deep\nimport { defineComponent, h } from 'vue'\n\nconst TreeNode = defineComponent({\n  name: 'TreeNode',\n  props: { node: { type: Object, required: true } },\n  setup(props) {\n    return () =&gt; h('li', [\n      h('span', props.node.label),\n      props.node.children?.length\n        ? h('ul', props.node.children.map(child =&gt;\n            h(TreeNode, { node: child, key: child.id }),   // recursion\n          ))\n        : null,\n    ])\n  },\n})</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Transforming slot children — impossible in a template:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// Adds a separator between every child the parent passed in\nimport { defineComponent, h, Fragment } from 'vue'\n\nexport default defineComponent({\n  props: { separator: { type: String, default: '·' } },\n  setup(props, { slots }) {\n    return () =&gt; {\n      const children = slots.default?.() ?? []\n      const withSeparators = children.flatMap((child, i) =&gt;\n        i === 0 ? [child] : [h('span', { class: 'sep' }, props.separator), child],\n      )\n      return h(Fragment, withSeparators)\n    }\n  },\n})</code></pre>`,
        `<p>JSX — the same thing, more readable:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-jsx">// vite.config.js: import vueJsx from '@vitejs/plugin-vue-jsx'\n//                 plugins: [vue(), vueJsx()]\nimport { defineComponent, ref } from 'vue'\n\nexport default defineComponent({\n  props: { items: Array },\n  setup(props) {\n    const selected = ref(null)\n\n    return () =&gt; (\n      &lt;ul class="list"&gt;\n        {props.items.map(item =&gt; (\n          &lt;li\n            key={item.id}\n            class={{ active: selected.value === item.id }}\n            onClick={() =&gt; (selected.value = item.id)}\n          &gt;\n            {item.name}\n          &lt;/li&gt;\n        ))}\n      &lt;/ul&gt;\n    )\n  },\n})</code></pre>`,
        `<h2>Common mistakes &amp; gotchas</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Returning a vnode instead of a function.</strong> <code>setup()</code> must return a <em>function</em> that returns vnodes, or the component renders once and never updates.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Passing slots as arrays.</strong> Named slots must be an object of functions; arrays are treated as default children and lose lazy evaluation.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Forgetting <code>key</code> in mapped children.</strong> Same consequences as a missing <code>:key</code> in <code>v-for</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Writing listeners as <code>click</code>.</strong> They are props: <code>onClick</code>, camelCase.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Expecting compiler optimisations.</strong> Hand-written render functions get no static hoisting, no patch flags, no block trees — they are diffed in full.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Reaching for JSX out of React habit.</strong> Templates are the Vue idiom, better optimised and better tooled; JSX is for the cases that genuinely need it.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Creating components inside the render function.</strong> A new component identity on every render means a full unmount/remount each time. Define them outside.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Mixing a <code>template</code> block and a render function.</strong> The render function wins and the template is silently ignored.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>Best practices</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Default to templates. Reach for a render function only when the markup's <em>structure</em> is dynamic in a way a template cannot express.</li>`,
        `<li>Use JSX rather than raw <code>h()</code> when a render function gets long — it is far more readable.</li>`,
        `<li>Keep render functions small; extract sub-trees into helper functions or child components.</li>`,
        `<li>Always key mapped children.</li>`,
        `<li>Define child components at module scope, never inside the render.</li>`,
        `<li>Prefer a template with <code>&lt;component :is&gt;</code> over a render function when that covers the dynamic case — it usually does.</li>`,
        `<li>Document why a component uses a render function, so the next reader does not "simplify" it back to a template.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>⭐ Interview lens</h2>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "When would you use a render function instead of a template?"</strong><br>A: When the structure itself is dynamic in a way templates can't express. The classic examples are a heading component whose tag depends on a <code>level</code> prop — a template needs six branches, a render function is one interpolated string — components that inspect or transform their slot children, recursive tree rendering, and schema-driven form generation. For everything else templates are better: more readable, and they get compiler optimisations a hand-written render function doesn't.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "What's the performance trade-off?"</strong><br>A: Templates are usually faster. Because they're statically analysable, the compiler hoists static nodes, attaches patch flags telling the runtime exactly which bindings can change, and groups dynamic nodes into flat block arrays. A hand-written render function is opaque to all of that, so it's diffed in full. People assume render functions are the "lower-level, faster" option and it's the other way round.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "How do templates map to <code>h()</code>?"</strong><br>A: <code>v-if</code> becomes a ternary returning <code>null</code> for the false branch. <code>v-for</code> becomes <code>.map()</code> with an explicit <code>key</code> in the props. Events become <code>on</code>-prefixed camelCase props — <code>@click</code> is <code>onClick</code> — and modifiers have no equivalent, so <code>.prevent</code> means calling <code>preventDefault()</code> yourself. <code>v-model</code> expands to a <code>value</code> prop plus an <code>onInput</code> handler. Multiple roots use <code>Fragment</code>, and named slots are passed as an object of functions.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Why must <code>setup()</code> return a function here?" (trap)</strong><br>A: Because the render function has to run on <em>every</em> render, inside the reactive effect, so its reads are tracked and re-read each time. <code>setup()</code> itself runs once per instance. If you return the vnode directly, you've created it once with the values from that moment, and it never updates — the component renders and then freezes with no error.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Is JSX in Vue the same as in React?"</strong><br>A: The syntax is nearly identical, and both compile to function calls creating elements. The differences are in the details: Vue's JSX compiles to <code>h()</code>, <code>class</code> stays <code>class</code> rather than <code>className</code> and accepts objects and arrays like a template binding, and <code>v-model</code> has a JSX form. It needs <code>@vitejs/plugin-vue-jsx</code>. But it's not the Vue idiom — templates are, and they optimise better.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<h2>Cheat-card</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><code>h(type, props, children)</code> — templates compile to exactly this.</li>`,
        `<li><code>setup()</code> must return a <strong>function</strong> returning vnodes, not a vnode.</li>`,
        `<li>Events = <code>onClick</code>, camelCase props. No modifier syntax.</li>`,
        `<li><code>v-if</code> → ternary (<code>null</code> for nothing) · <code>v-for</code> → <code>.map()</code> + <code>key</code> · <code>v-model</code> → value + handler.</li>`,
        `<li>Multiple roots → <code>Fragment</code>. Named slots → <strong>object of functions</strong>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>No compiler optimisations</strong> — no hoisting, no patch flags, no blocks. Templates are usually faster.</li>`,
        `<li>Use for: dynamic tag names, transforming slot children, recursion, schema-driven markup.</li>`,
        `<li>JSX via <code>@vitejs/plugin-vue-jsx</code> — same output, nicer syntax for long render functions.</li>`,
        `<li>Never define a component inside a render function.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
      ].join('\n'),
    },

    {
      title: 'TypeScript with Vue',
      slug: 'typescript-vue',
      order: 2,
      content: [
        `<h2>Why it matters</h2>`,
        `<p>TypeScript is the default for new Vue projects, and it is one of the main reasons the Composition API exists — the Options API's reliance on <code>this</code> and merged option objects made inference fragile, while Composition API code is ordinary functions and variables that TypeScript already handles well.</p>`,
        `<p>Interviewers ask because typing a component well requires knowing several Vue-specific mechanisms — the generic forms of <code>defineProps</code> and <code>defineEmits</code>, typing refs and template refs, and <code>InjectionKey</code>. These are small APIs that immediately show whether someone has written typed Vue in anger.</p>`,
        `<h2>The concept, simply</h2>`,
        `<p>Most Vue TypeScript is just TypeScript. The Vue-specific parts are a short list:</p>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><code>defineProps&lt;T&gt;()</code> / <code>withDefaults</code> — props typed by an interface rather than a runtime object.</li>`,
        `<li><code>defineEmits&lt;T&gt;()</code> — event names and payloads checked at compile time.</li>`,
        `<li><code>ref&lt;T&gt;()</code> — explicit when inference is not enough, particularly for nullable values.</li>`,
        `<li><code>InjectionKey&lt;T&gt;</code> — carries the type from <code>provide</code> to <code>inject</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><code>PropType&lt;T&gt;</code> — for complex types in the runtime (Options API) form.</li>`,
        `<li>Component instance types — <code>InstanceType&lt;typeof MyComponent&gt;</code> for template refs.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<p>Two toolchain pieces make it work: <code>lang="ts"</code> on the script block, and the <strong>Vue - Official</strong> (formerly Volar) extension, which type-checks templates as well as scripts — meaning a typo in a template binding is an error, not a runtime <code>undefined</code>.</p>`,
        `<h2>How it works</h2>`,
        `<p><strong>Type-only props</strong> are compiled away: <code>defineProps&lt;Props&gt;()</code> generates the runtime declaration from the type at build time. That gives you a single source of truth — the interface documents the component and checks its callers. Defaults need <code>withDefaults</code>, and object or array defaults still need factory functions for the same instance-sharing reason as before. (In 3.5+ you can also destructure props with defaults directly, which the compiler transforms.)</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Typed emits</strong> use a call-signature or tuple syntax that catches both a misspelled event name and a wrong payload shape at compile time. Runtime validators can only warn in development, so this is a genuine upgrade rather than a convenience.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Refs and nullability.</strong> <code>ref(null)</code> infers <code>Ref&lt;null&gt;</code>, so assigning a user to it errors. <code>ref&lt;User | null&gt;(null)</code> states the intent and forces you to handle the null case — which is the point. Template refs follow the same rule and start as <code>null</code> until mount, so the non-null assertion belongs in <code>onMounted</code>, not at the top of setup.</p>`,
        `<p><strong><code>InjectionKey&lt;T&gt;</code></strong> is a <code>Symbol</code> carrying a phantom type: <code>provide</code> checks the value matches and <code>inject</code> returns the right type automatically, with no casting. It also solves the string-collision problem, so it is worth doing for both reasons.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Template type-checking</strong> is what elevates the whole experience. With the Vue - Official extension and <code>vue-tsc</code> in CI, <code>{{ user.nmae }}</code> fails the build. Running <code>vue-tsc --noEmit</code> alongside the build is the standard setup.</p>`,
        `<h2>Code examples</h2>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;script setup lang="ts"&gt;\nimport { ref, computed, onMounted } from 'vue'\nimport type { Ref } from 'vue'\n\n// ── Props: an interface is the single source of truth ──\ninterface User {\n  id: number\n  name: string\n  email: string\n  role: 'admin' | 'editor' | 'viewer'\n}\n\ninterface Props {\n  user: User\n  count?: number\n  tags?: string[]\n  variant?: 'primary' | 'danger'\n}\n\nconst props = withDefaults(defineProps&lt;Props&gt;(), {\n  count: 0,\n  tags: () =&gt; [],          // still a factory for arrays/objects\n  variant: 'primary',\n})\n\n// ── Emits: names AND payloads checked at compile time ──\nconst emit = defineEmits&lt;{\n  select: [id: number]\n  update: [user: User, source: string]\n  close: []\n}&gt;()\n\nemit('select', 42)         // ✅\n// emit('select', '42')    // ❌ Argument of type 'string' is not assignable\n// emit('selct', 42)       // ❌ No overload matches this call\n\n// ── Refs ──\nconst count = ref(0)                       // inferred Ref&lt;number&gt;\nconst user = ref&lt;User | null&gt;(null)        // explicit — null is a real state\nconst users: Ref&lt;User[]&gt; = ref([])\n\n// Computeds infer from the getter\nconst adminCount = computed(() =&gt; users.value.filter(u =&gt; u.role === 'admin').length)\n\n// ── Template refs ──\nconst inputEl = ref&lt;HTMLInputElement | null&gt;(null)\nonMounted(() =&gt; {\n  inputEl.value?.focus()                   // null until mounted — optional chain\n})\n&lt;/script&gt;\n\n&lt;template&gt;\n  &lt;input ref="inputEl"&gt;\n  &lt;!-- With the Vue - Official extension, this is a compile error: --&gt;\n  &lt;!-- &lt;p&gt;{{ user.nmae }}&lt;/p&gt; --&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-typescript">// ── provide / inject with InjectionKey ──\n// keys.ts\nimport type { InjectionKey, Ref } from 'vue'\n\nexport interface AuthContext {\n  user: Readonly&lt;Ref&lt;User | null&gt;&gt;\n  login: (email: string, password: string) =&gt; Promise&lt;void&gt;\n  logout: () =&gt; void\n}\n\nexport const authKey = Symbol('auth') as InjectionKey&lt;AuthContext&gt;\n\n// Provider\nprovide(authKey, { user: readonly(user), login, logout })   // ✅ shape checked\n\n// Consumer — type flows through, no cast needed\nconst auth = inject(authKey)        // AuthContext | undefined\nif (!auth) throw new Error('AuthProvider missing')\nauth.login('a@b.com', 'pw')         // ✅ fully typed</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-typescript">// ── Typed composables ──\nimport { ref, toValue, type Ref, type MaybeRefOrGetter } from 'vue'\n\ninterface UseFetchReturn&lt;T&gt; {\n  data: Ref&lt;T | null&gt;\n  error: Ref&lt;Error | null&gt;\n  loading: Ref&lt;boolean&gt;\n  refetch: () =&gt; Promise&lt;void&gt;\n}\n\nexport function useFetch&lt;T&gt;(url: MaybeRefOrGetter&lt;string&gt;): UseFetchReturn&lt;T&gt; {\n  const data = ref(null) as Ref&lt;T | null&gt;\n  const error = ref&lt;Error | null&gt;(null)\n  const loading = ref(false)\n\n  async function refetch() {\n    loading.value = true\n    try {\n      const res = await fetch(toValue(url))\n      data.value = (await res.json()) as T\n    } catch (e) {\n      error.value = e as Error\n    } finally {\n      loading.value = false\n    }\n  }\n\n  return { data, error, loading, refetch }\n}\n\n// Callers get a fully typed result\nconst { data } = useFetch&lt;User[]&gt;('/api/users')   // Ref&lt;User[] | null&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-typescript">// ── Component instance types, for template refs on components ──\nimport ModalDialog from './ModalDialog.vue'\n\nconst modal = ref&lt;InstanceType&lt;typeof ModalDialog&gt; | null&gt;(null)\nmodal.value?.open()          // ✅ only what defineExpose published\n\n// ── Options API form, when you need it ──\nimport { defineComponent, type PropType } from 'vue'\n\nexport default defineComponent({\n  props: {\n    user: { type: Object as PropType&lt;User&gt;, required: true },\n    variant: { type: String as PropType&lt;'primary' | 'danger'&gt;, default: 'primary' },\n  },\n})\n\n// ── Typed Pinia store — inference is automatic with the setup syntax ──\nexport const useAuthStore = defineStore('auth', () =&gt; {\n  const user = ref&lt;User | null&gt;(null)\n  const isLoggedIn = computed(() =&gt; user.value !== null)\n  async function login(email: string, password: string): Promise&lt;void&gt; { /* … */ }\n  return { user, isLoggedIn, login }\n})\n// useAuthStore().user  →  Ref&lt;User | null&gt;, no annotations needed</code></pre>`,
        `<h2>Common mistakes &amp; gotchas</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong><code>ref(null)</code> for something that will hold an object.</strong> Infers <code>Ref&lt;null&gt;</code> and every assignment errors. Use <code>ref&lt;T | null&gt;(null)</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Non-null assertion on a template ref during setup.</strong> <code>inputEl.value!.focus()</code> compiles and crashes — the ref is genuinely null until mount.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Mixing type-only and runtime prop declarations.</strong> <code>defineProps</code> takes one or the other, not both.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Referencing a local type in <code>defineProps</code> that the compiler cannot resolve.</strong> Older versions required the interface to be in the same file; imported types are supported in recent versions but complex generics can still confuse it.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Skipping <code>withDefaults</code></strong> and then treating optional props as defined.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Casting away <code>inject</code>'s <code>undefined</code>.</strong> The union is telling you the provider might be missing; throw a clear error instead of asserting.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Not running <code>vue-tsc</code> in CI.</strong> Editor errors are not build errors; without it, type failures reach production.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Using the old Vetur extension.</strong> Superseded by Vue - Official (Volar); the two conflict.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Typing a store's state manually</strong> when the setup syntax already infers it.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>Best practices</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Use <code>&lt;script setup lang="ts"&gt;</code> with type-only <code>defineProps</code>/<code>defineEmits</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Define shared domain types (<code>User</code>, <code>Product</code>) in one <code>types/</code> module and import them everywhere.</li>`,
        `<li>Make nullability explicit: <code>ref&lt;T | null&gt;(null)</code> and handle the null branch.</li>`,
        `<li>Use <code>InjectionKey&lt;T&gt;</code> for every provide/inject pair — types and collision-safety in one move.</li>`,
        `<li>Type composable returns with an interface so consumers see a clear contract.</li>`,
        `<li>Run <code>vue-tsc --noEmit</code> in CI alongside the build.</li>`,
        `<li>Enable <code>strict</code> in <code>tsconfig.json</code>; anything less gives up most of the value.</li>`,
        `<li>Install the Vue - Official extension so templates are type-checked, not just scripts.</li>`,
        `<li>Let Pinia's setup syntax infer store types rather than annotating by hand.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>⭐ Interview lens</h2>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "How do you type props and emits?"</strong><br>A: With the generic forms. <code>defineProps&lt;Props&gt;()</code> takes an interface and the compiler generates the runtime declaration from it, so the type is the single source of truth; <code>withDefaults</code> supplies defaults, with factory functions for objects and arrays. <code>defineEmits&lt;{ select: [id: number] }&gt;()</code> checks both the event name and the payload at compile time — a real improvement over runtime validators, which only warn in development and are stripped from production.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Why does the Composition API type better than the Options API?"</strong><br>A: Because it's ordinary functions and variables. The Options API asks TypeScript to infer types across a merged object of <code>data</code>, <code>computed</code>, <code>methods</code> and <code>props</code>, all reachable through <code>this</code> — solvable but fragile, and it needed <code>defineComponent</code> plus <code>PropType</code> annotations to work at all. Composition API code has no <code>this</code>: a <code>ref</code> is a typed variable and a composable is a typed function, so inference is whatever TypeScript already does well.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "How do you type <code>provide</code>/<code>inject</code>?"</strong><br>A: <code>InjectionKey&lt;T&gt;</code> — a <code>Symbol</code> cast to that type and exported from a shared module. <code>provide(key, value)</code> then checks the value's shape and <code>inject(key)</code> returns <code>T | undefined</code> with no cast. The <code>undefined</code> is deliberate: it's telling you no provider might exist, so I throw a descriptive error rather than asserting it away. Using a Symbol also removes the string-collision risk.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "<code>const el = ref&lt;HTMLInputElement | null&gt;(null)</code> — why not just assert non-null?" (trap)</strong><br>A: Because it genuinely <em>is</em> null until the component mounts, and null again if the element sits inside a <code>v-if</code> that's false. A non-null assertion silences the compiler and moves the failure to runtime. The correct handling is to read it inside <code>onMounted</code> and use optional chaining, which is exactly what the type is prompting you to do.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Are Vue templates type-checked?"</strong><br>A: Yes, with the Vue - Official extension (formerly Volar) in the editor and <code>vue-tsc</code> on the command line. It type-checks bindings, so <code>{{ user.nmae }}</code> is an error rather than a silent <code>undefined</code>, and it checks props passed to child components against their declared types. The important part is running <code>vue-tsc --noEmit</code> in CI — editor squiggles aren't a build gate, and without it type errors ship.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<h2>Cheat-card</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><code>&lt;script setup lang="ts"&gt;</code> + <strong>Vue - Official (Volar)</strong> + <code>vue-tsc --noEmit</code> in CI.</li>`,
        `<li><code>defineProps&lt;Props&gt;()</code> + <code>withDefaults</code> (factories for object/array defaults).</li>`,
        `<li><code>defineEmits&lt;{ select: [id: number] }&gt;()</code> — names and payloads checked.</li>`,
        `<li><code>ref&lt;T | null&gt;(null)</code> — never <code>ref(null)</code> for objects.</li>`,
        `<li>Template refs are <code>null</code> until mount → optional chaining in <code>onMounted</code>, not <code>!</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><code>InjectionKey&lt;T&gt;</code> = typed + collision-safe provide/inject; returns <code>T | undefined</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Component refs: <code>ref&lt;InstanceType&lt;typeof Comp&gt; | null&gt;(null)</code> — sees only <code>defineExpose</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Options API: <code>defineComponent</code> + <code>Object as PropType&lt;T&gt;</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Pinia setup stores infer everything — don't annotate by hand.</li>`,
        `<li>Composition API types better because there's no <code>this</code>.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
      ].join('\n'),
    },

    {
      title: 'Security in Vue Applications',
      slug: 'security',
      order: 3,
      content: [
        `<h2>Why it matters</h2>`,
        `<p>Vue escapes output by default, which handles the most common XSS vector automatically — and lulls people into thinking security is handled. It is not. <code>v-html</code>, dynamic <code>:href</code>, dynamic component names, and client-side "authorisation" are all live holes, and every one of them appears in real Vue codebases.</p>`,
        `<p>Interviewers ask because security questions separate people who have shipped software with real users from people who have not. The single most important thing you can demonstrate is knowing that <strong>client-side checks are never the security boundary</strong>.</p>`,
        `<h2>The concept, simply</h2>`,
        `<p>The threats that actually matter in a Vue front end:</p>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>XSS</strong> — attacker-controlled content executing as code in your page. The dominant front-end vulnerability.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Token theft</strong> — credentials stored somewhere a script can read them, which turns any XSS into account takeover.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>CSRF</strong> — another site making authenticated requests on the user's behalf.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Client-side authorisation</strong> — hiding UI and believing that protects the underlying data.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Dependency and secret leakage</strong> — a compromised package, or an API key bundled into client JavaScript.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `{{fig:ch9-security.svg|Vue security surface map: escaped interpolation as the safe default, with v-html, dynamic href, dynamic component is, localStorage tokens and client-side guards flagged as risk points|Vue escapes by default; every hole is somewhere you deliberately stepped outside that default.}}`,
        `<h2>How it works</h2>`,
        `<p><strong>XSS and Vue's default.</strong> <code>{{ }}</code> converts to a string and HTML-escapes it, so markup renders as visible text. Attribute bindings are escaped too. That covers most output. The exits from safety are:</p>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong><code>v-html</code></strong> — sets <code>innerHTML</code>. Any user-influenced string here is stored XSS. If you genuinely need rich HTML, sanitise with DOMPurify at render time, and prefer sanitising on the server as well.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Dynamic <code>:href</code> / <code>:src</code></strong> — <code>javascript:alert(1)</code> in a bound href executes on click. Vue does not filter URL schemes; validate that the URL starts with <code>http:</code>, <code>https:</code> or <code>mailto:</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Dynamic <code>:is</code>, dynamic style, and injected templates</strong> — never let user input choose a component or reach a template compiler.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<p><strong>Token storage.</strong> <code>localStorage</code> is readable by any JavaScript running on the page, including anything injected via XSS or shipped by a compromised dependency. An httpOnly, Secure, SameSite cookie is not readable by script, so an XSS cannot exfiltrate it. The trade-off is that cookies are sent automatically, which reintroduces CSRF — handled with <code>SameSite=Lax</code> or <code>Strict</code> plus a CSRF token for state-changing requests.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Client-side authorisation is UX.</strong> A route guard, a <code>v-if="isAdmin"</code>, or a <code>v-permission</code> directive decides what to <em>render</em> in a browser the user fully controls — they can edit the JavaScript, call the API directly, or replay requests. The server must authorise every request independently. Worse, hiding a section client-side does not hide the data if it was already included in an API response.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Secrets.</strong> Anything in a Vite <code>VITE_</code>-prefixed environment variable is inlined into the bundle and public. API keys with real privileges belong on a server; the client can only hold values that are safe to publish.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Defence in depth:</strong> a Content Security Policy limits what an XSS can do even if one lands; Subresource Integrity protects against a compromised CDN; and <code>npm audit</code> plus lockfile review addresses supply chain.</p>`,
        `<h2>Code examples</h2>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;script setup&gt;\nimport DOMPurify from 'dompurify'\nimport { computed } from 'vue'\n\nconst props = defineProps({ comment: String, article: Object })\n\n// ✅ If raw HTML is genuinely required, sanitise it — with an allow-list\nconst safeHtml = computed(() =&gt;\n  DOMPurify.sanitize(props.article.body, {\n    ALLOWED_TAGS: ['p', 'strong', 'em', 'a', 'ul', 'ol', 'li', 'h2', 'h3', 'code', 'pre'],\n    ALLOWED_ATTR: ['href', 'title'],\n  }),\n)\n&lt;/script&gt;\n\n&lt;template&gt;\n  &lt;!-- ✅ Escaped — safe with any input --&gt;\n  &lt;p&gt;{{ comment }}&lt;/p&gt;\n\n  &lt;!-- ❌ Stored XSS if \`comment\` came from a user --&gt;\n  &lt;div v-html="comment"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;\n\n  &lt;!-- ✅ Sanitised --&gt;\n  &lt;div v-html="safeHtml"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// URL injection — Vue does NOT filter schemes for you\nconst DANGEROUS = /^\\s*(javascript|data|vbscript):/i\n\nfunction safeUrl(url) {\n  if (typeof url !== 'string' || DANGEROUS.test(url)) return '#'\n  return url\n}\n\n// ❌ &lt;a :href=\"user.website\"&gt;  →  href=\"javascript:steal()\" executes on click\n// ✅ &lt;a :href=\"safeUrl(user.website)\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\"&gt;\n\n// Same class of problem elsewhere:\n// ❌ &lt;component :is=\"userSuppliedName\" /&gt;      — arbitrary component instantiation\n// ❌ &lt;div :style=\"userSuppliedStyle\"&gt;          — CSS injection\n// ❌ new Function(userInput) / eval(userInput)</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// ── Token storage ──\n\n// ❌ Readable by any script on the page. One XSS = full account takeover.\nlocalStorage.setItem('token', jwt)\n\n// ✅ The server sets an httpOnly cookie; JavaScript can never read it\n//    Set-Cookie: token=…; HttpOnly; Secure; SameSite=Lax; Path=/\naxios.defaults.withCredentials = true    // cookie sent automatically\n\n// Cookies are auto-sent ⇒ CSRF protection needed for state-changing requests\naxios.interceptors.request.use((config) =&gt; {\n  if (['post', 'put', 'patch', 'delete'].includes(config.method)) {\n    config.headers['X-CSRF-Token'] = getCsrfTokenFromMeta()\n  }\n  return config\n})\n\n// If you must hold a token in memory (SPA + short-lived access token),\n// keep it in a module variable rather than localStorage, and refresh via\n// an httpOnly refresh-token cookie.</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// ── Authorisation: client-side is UX only ──\n\n// Route guard — improves UX, protects nothing\nrouter.beforeEach((to) =&gt; {\n  if (to.meta.requiresAdmin &amp;&amp; !auth.isAdmin) return { name: 'forbidden' }\n})\n\n// &lt;button v-if=\"auth.isAdmin\"&gt;Delete&lt;/button&gt;\n//   → the user can re-add the button from devtools, or just:\n//     fetch('/api/users/1', { method: 'DELETE' })\n//\n// ✅ The server checks on EVERY request:\n//     if (!req.user.isAdmin) return res.status(403).end()\n//\n// ⚠️ And don't send data the user isn't allowed to see and then hide it\n//    in the UI — it's in the network tab either way.</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// ── Secrets and CSP ──\n\n// ❌ Inlined into the bundle at build time — publicly readable\n// .env:  VITE_STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=sk_live_…\nconst key = import.meta.env.VITE_STRIPE_SECRET_KEY\n\n// ✅ Only publishable values belong in the client\nconst key = import.meta.env.VITE_STRIPE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY\n// Anything with real privileges is used server-side only.\n\n// A CSP limits what an XSS can do even if one lands.\n// Content-Security-Policy:\n//   default-src 'self';\n//   script-src 'self';\n//   style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline';\n//   img-src 'self' data: https:;\n//   connect-src 'self' https://api.example.com;\n//   frame-ancestors 'none';\n// Note: Vue's runtime compiler needs 'unsafe-eval' — another reason to use\n// the runtime-only build with precompiled templates, which is Vite's default.</code></pre>`,
        `<h2>Common mistakes &amp; gotchas</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong><code>v-html</code> with user content.</strong> The single most common Vue XSS. Sanitise, or do not use it.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Sanitising once and storing the result.</strong> Sanitiser rules improve over time; sanitise at render as well as on input.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Unvalidated <code>:href</code>.</strong> <code>javascript:</code> URLs execute. Vue does not filter schemes.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Tokens in <code>localStorage</code>.</strong> Turns any XSS — including one from a compromised dependency — into account takeover.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Believing route guards protect data.</strong> They control rendering only.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Sending over-broad API responses</strong> and hiding fields in the UI. The data is in the network tab.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Secrets in <code>VITE_</code> variables.</strong> Anything prefixed that way is inlined and public.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Trusting client-side validation.</strong> It is UX; the server must validate independently.</li>`,
        `<li><strong><code>target="_blank"</code> without <code>rel="noopener"</code>.</strong> The opened page can manipulate <code>window.opener</code> in older browsers.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Ignoring dependency audits.</strong> A malicious postinstall script or a compromised transitive dependency runs with your build's privileges.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>Best practices</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Default to <code>{{ }}</code>. Treat every <code>v-html</code> as requiring justification and sanitisation.</li>`,
        `<li>Sanitise with DOMPurify using an explicit allow-list, at render time.</li>`,
        `<li>Validate URL schemes before binding to <code>:href</code> or <code>:src</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Store session tokens in httpOnly, Secure, SameSite cookies; never in <code>localStorage</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Pair cookie auth with CSRF tokens on state-changing requests.</li>`,
        `<li>Enforce authorisation server-side on every request; treat guards and <code>v-if</code> as UX.</li>`,
        `<li>Return only data the user is allowed to see — do not filter in the UI.</li>`,
        `<li>Keep real secrets on the server; only publishable keys in client env vars.</li>`,
        `<li>Ship a Content Security Policy and use the runtime-only Vue build so you do not need <code>unsafe-eval</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Run <code>npm audit</code> in CI, pin versions with a lockfile, and review new dependencies.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>⭐ Interview lens</h2>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "How does Vue protect against XSS, and where does that protection stop?"</strong><br>A: <code>{{ }}</code> and attribute bindings are HTML-escaped, so markup from user data renders as visible text rather than executing — that covers the common case automatically. It stops wherever you deliberately opt out: <code>v-html</code>, which sets <code>innerHTML</code> and is straight stored XSS on user content; dynamic <code>:href</code> or <code>:src</code>, since Vue doesn't filter <code>javascript:</code> URLs; dynamic <code>:is</code>; and anything reaching a runtime template compiler or <code>eval</code>.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Where do you store a JWT?"</strong><br>A: Not in <code>localStorage</code>. It's readable by any script on the page, so one XSS — including one from a compromised npm dependency — becomes full account takeover. An httpOnly, Secure, SameSite cookie can't be read by JavaScript at all. The trade-off is that cookies are sent automatically, which reintroduces CSRF, so pair it with <code>SameSite=Lax</code> or <code>Strict</code> and a CSRF token on state-changing requests. If a token must live in JS, keep it in a module variable rather than persistent storage, refreshed via an httpOnly refresh cookie.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Are route guards a security control?" (trap)</strong><br>A: No. They decide which component renders in a browser the user fully controls — they can edit the JavaScript, re-add a hidden button from devtools, or skip the UI entirely and call the API. Authorisation has to be enforced on the server, on every request. The related mistake is returning data the user shouldn't see and hiding it in the UI: it's visible in the network tab regardless.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "You need to render user-submitted rich text. How?"</strong><br>A: Sanitise with DOMPurify using an explicit allow-list of tags and attributes — permit <code>p</code>, <code>strong</code>, <code>a</code>, lists and headings, and nothing that carries script: no <code>script</code>, no <code>on*</code> handlers, no <code>iframe</code>, no <code>style</code>. Sanitise at render time rather than only on input, because sanitiser rules improve and stored content was sanitised by an older version. Ideally sanitise server-side as well. And where markdown is enough, render it to a restricted subset instead of accepting raw HTML at all.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Can you put an API key in a Vue app?"</strong><br>A: Only one that's safe to publish. Vite inlines every <code>VITE_</code>-prefixed variable into the bundle, so it's readable by anyone who opens the source — there's no such thing as a hidden client-side secret. Publishable keys like Stripe's are designed for that; anything with real privileges has to be used from a server that the client calls. If a key leaked this way, rotating it is the only fix.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<h2>Cheat-card</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><code>{{ }}</code> escapes — safe by default. <code>v-html</code> does not.</li>`,
        `<li>Sanitise with <strong>DOMPurify</strong> + allow-list, <strong>at render time</strong>.</li>`,
        `<li>Vue does <strong>not</strong> filter <code>javascript:</code> in <code>:href</code>/<code>:src</code> — validate the scheme.</li>`,
        `<li>Never <code>:is</code>, <code>:style</code> or template compilation from user input.</li>`,
        `<li>Tokens → <strong>httpOnly + Secure + SameSite cookie</strong>, never <code>localStorage</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Cookie auth ⇒ add <strong>CSRF tokens</strong> on POST/PUT/PATCH/DELETE.</li>`,
        `<li>Guards, <code>v-if</code> and <code>v-permission</code> are <strong>UX, not security</strong> — authorise server-side every request.</li>`,
        `<li>Don't send data you then hide in the UI.</li>`,
        `<li><code>VITE_*</code> vars are <strong>public</strong> — publishable keys only.</li>`,
        `<li>Ship a <strong>CSP</strong>; use the runtime-only build to avoid <code>unsafe-eval</code>. <code>npm audit</code> in CI.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
      ].join('\n'),
    },
  ],
}
